    {
      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 3691.3,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 822,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound made of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than a simple chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the very foundation of life as we know it. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it matters:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Three States of Matter\nWater is famous for being the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** What we normally think of as water (rivers, oceans, rain, drinking water).\n*   **Solid:** Ice (glaciers, snow, ice cubes).\n*   **Gas:** Water vapor or steam (humidity in the air, clouds, fog).\n\n### 2. Unique Chemical Properties\nWater has several bizarre and unique scientific properties that make it unlike almost any other liquid on Earth:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of the way its atoms are electrically charged (polar), water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it can easily carry nutrients, minerals, and chemicals through our bodies and through the Earth's ecosystems.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water is different; when it freezes into ice, it expands and becomes *less* dense. This is why ice floats on water. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the entire planet and keeps human bodies from overheating.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of giant trees).\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas.\n*   **Scarcity:** Despite the Earth looking like a \"blue marble,\" **96.5% of Earth's water is saltwater** in the oceans. Only about **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use (in lakes, rivers, and streams).\n*   **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly moving in a closed loop called the water cycle. It evaporates from the ocean into the air, forms clouds, falls back to Earth as rain or snow, and flows through rivers back to the ocean. The water you drink today is the exact same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\n### 4. Water and Life\nWater is the essential ingredient for life. \n*   **Biology:** The human body is made of roughly **60% water**. Our brains are about 73% water, and our lungs are about 83%. We can survive for weeks without food, but only a few days without water.\n*   **Ecosystems:** Every known living thing—from microscopic bacteria to giant blue whales—relies on water to survive, regulate temperature, and carry out chemical reactions within cells.\n*   **Habitability:** Astrobiologists searching for life on other planets always start with one primary question: *\"Is there water?\"* Where there is liquid water, there is a strong possibility of life. \n\nIn short, water is the driving force of nature, the cradle of biology, and the most precious resource on our planet."
